{"name":"Cadmium Blue","hex":"#0a1195","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cadmium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical element (symbol Cd) with an atomic number of 48: a soft, silvery-white metal.","A single atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cadmium"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cadmium","blocks":[{"text":"Cadmium is a chemical element; it has symbol Cd and atomic number 48. This soft, silvery-white metal is chemically similar to the two other stable metals in group 12, zinc and mercury. Like zinc, it demonstrates oxidation state +2 in most of its compounds, and like mercury, it has a lower melting point than the transition metals in groups 3 through 11. Cadmium and its congeners in group 12 are often not considered transition metals, in that they do not have partly filled d or f electron shells in the elemental or common oxidation states. The average concentration of cadmium in Earth's crust is between 0.1 and 0.5 parts per million (ppm). It was discovered in 1817 simultaneously by Stromeyer and Hermann, both in Germany, as an impurity in zinc carbonate."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Cadmium-crystal_bar.jpg/330px-Cadmium-crystal_bar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadmium","wikidata":"Q1091"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Cadmium_cutoff.png/500px-Cadmium_cutoff.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Cadmium_cutoff.png/500px-Cadmium_cutoff.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The cadmium-113 total cross section clearly showing the cadmium cut-off","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACadmium_cutoff.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg/330px-Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg/330px-Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Friedrich Stromeyer","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFriedrich_Stromeyer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/CadmiumMetalUSGOV.jpg/330px-CadmiumMetalUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wiki